  "strategy": "1. **Approach and Rationale**  \n   The Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem requires finding a step function **h** on [0, 2] that minimizes the maximum overlap integral **C5**, subject to **h(x) \u2208 [0, 1]** and **\u222bh(x)dx = 1**. The previous approach used gradient descent and discrete optimization, but these methods often get stuck in local optima. We propose a **convex duality-based reformulation** with **first-order methods** that guarantees global optimality for the relaxation. Specifically:  \n   - Transform the non-convex problem into a **convex duality formulation** by relaxing the nonlinear objectives.  \n   - Use **Nesterov's accelerated gradient method** for efficient optimization.  \n   - Compare three duality relaxations:  \n     (i) **Box-constrained** relaxation (0 \u2264 h \u2264 1).  \n     (ii) **Absolute value-constrained** relaxation (\u22121 \u2264 h - 1 \u2264 0).  \n     (iii) **Constraint-free** relaxation (unbounded domain).  \n   - Validate solutions by projecting onto the original constraint set to ensure feasibility.  \n   - We hypothesize that tighter duality constraints will converge faster to the global minimum.  \n\n2. **Mathematical Specification**  \n   - **Variables**: Define **h** as a Lagrangian variable for dual variables.  \n   - **Objective**: Minimize **C5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1 - h(x+k))dx** via **dual L2 regularization**.  \n   - **Constraints**:  \n     Primary: 0 \u2264 h(i) \u2264 1 (discrete box).  \n     Secondary: \u2211h(i) = n_points/2 (discrete integral).  \n   - **Duality**: Use **Lagrange multipliers** for constraint relaxation, forming a convex program amenable to fast solvers (ECOS, CVXOPT).  \n   - **Solver Method**: ADMM with backtracking line search for **sparse solutions**.  \n\n3. **Implementation Procedure**  \n   - **Start**: Use `initial_h_values` (if available) or generate by `thue_morse`/`random_binary_balanced`.  \n   - **Phase 1: Duality Formation**:  \n     Relax **C5** and **integral** constraints via **dual penalization**. Saddle-point solve the Lagrangian.  \n     Soft-dual feasible iteration: `h_new = h - learning_rate * (\u2207f + \u03bb\u2202g)`.  \n   - **Phase 2: Optimization**:  \n     Use **Adam-style optimization** for dual variables. Include **entropy residual penalty** to stabilize convergence.  \n   - **Phase 3: Validation**:  \n     Project dual solution back to primal space with `project_onto_box_sum`. Check feasibility (sum(h) \u2248 n_points/2).  \n   - **Termination**: Stop when **dual gap** < 1e-04 or time exceeded.  \n\n4. **Search Choices and Variations**  \n   - **Base**: Duality with **L2 penalty** (least squares).  \n   - **Variation 1**: **L1 prefix** on binary constraints for sparsity.  \n   - **Variation 2**: **Multi-scale duality** (combine box and integral normalizations).  \n   - **Parameter Sweep**: Optimize penalty parameter \u03bb \u2208 [0.1, 10] every N_iter to $$ 0 \\text{ to } 10. $$ Clear all temporary files.  \n\n5. **Numerical Robustness**  \n   - **Fallback**: If infeasible, use `random_binary_balanced` as seed.  \n   - **Gradient Handling**: Compute dual gradients via finite-differences for user-defined parameters.  \n   - **Sample Size**: 1000-2000 storage per function.  \n\n6. **Runtime Safeguard**  \n   - Split budget across three scales:  \n     - Fast approximation (duality) - 200s  \n     - Refinement - 800s  \n     min_iter = (time_remaining) / 0.5;  \n   - Return upon **budget exhaustion**.  \n   - Enforcement of **equality constraints** via cross-entropy method.",
